  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite water vapor imagery shows a upper level wave \ndeparting North Dakota northeast into Canada. This system has pushed \na cold front southward into western Kansas early this morning which \nwill continue to move slowly southeast towards central Kansas today. \nScattered showers and few embedded weak storms look to linger this \nmorning across central Kansas into parts of south central Kansas. \nHot temperatures will affect locations south of the cold front today \nwith upper 90s to near 100 expected, and not as hot for locations \nnorth of the front in central Kansas. A few storms look to redevelop \nlater this afternoon/evening along the frontal boundary over central \nKansas. Limited instability and high inverted-V type soundings \nsupports gusty downburst winds from any stronger updraft(severe \nthreat is low). Low level jet increases tonight with the main focus \nfrom this feature favoring more numerous showers/storms developing \nover eastern Nebraska into Iowa overnight. Current satellite imagery \nalso shows another upper level wave embedded in the strong westerly \nflow over the northern Rockies with a second system diving southeast \nacross western Canada. This first wave looks to spark off additional \nshowers/storms late tonight over western Nebraska/northwest Kansas \nfrom modest 700mb warm air advection. This scattered activity will \ndrift slowly southeast across Kansas on Wednesday with surface front \nalso moving slowly southeast across the region. The cloud coverage \nand lingering activity could limit instability build-up over \ncentral/south central Kansas and lower daytime highs. Meanwhile \nthere is some potential for higher instability to materialize over \nsoutheast Kansas, and combine with increasing mid-level winds from \napproaching second upper level wave. This could yield a few strong \nto marginally severe storms over southeast Kansas late Wednesday \nafternoon/evening. \n\nStorms look to increase in coverage for Wednesday night north of the \nsurface front where 700mb warm advection from the models favors \nelevated showers/storms. A few strong to marginally severe storms \nare possible given combination of elevated instability with \nfavorable directional wind shear(700-500mb layer) and increased mid-\nlevel flow. Heading into Thursday daytime highs could come in a \nlittle cooler than current forecast, especially with surface front \nprojected to be stalled near or south of the Kansas/Oklahoma border. \nShowers/storms could increase for Thursday night with 700mb warm \nadvection commencing north of the surface front. \n\nModels remain consistent with upper level high pressure center \nexpanding slowly eastward from the Rockies out into the plains for \nthe weekend into early next week. This will cause the heat dome and \ndry weather to build over Kansas.",
